WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.160
There are a million and one different ways
to praise a movie.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.280
Attendees of Hollywood premieres and festival
screenings will often stand right up in the

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.100
theater and cheer as the credits roll.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.660
Here are a few movies that earned themselves
a standing ovation when they hit the big screen.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:19.300
Nearly 30 years ago, Spike Lee took the film
world by storm when his uncompromising racial

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.920
drama Do the Right Thing made its premiere
at the Cannes Film Festival.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.060
Since then, the in-your-face director proved
himself as one of cinema's most gifted, if

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:31.730
often divisive, filmmakers by releasing a
steady stream of artistically ambitious and

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:32.960
politically-themed movies.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.829
In 2018, Lee took Cannes by storm with the
timely, racially-charged BlacKkKlansman, the

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:44.199
true story of an African American police officer
who infiltrated the Ku Klux Klan in the 1970s.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.380
"There's never been a black cop in this city.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.789
We think you might be the man to open things
up around here."

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:53.280
Simply put, BlacKkKlansman is a film that
refuses to be ignored.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.420
According to Variety, the usually stuffy Cannes
crowd seemed more than happy to give the film

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:03.059
its due, awarding it with a six-minute long
standing ovation, as well as the festival's

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.240
Grand Jury Prize.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.659
The other flick to get the haughty crowd at
the 2018 Cannes Film Festival to its feet

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.480
was, of all things, a remake of a horror movie.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.850
It helps, of course, that this remake was
directed by Luca Guadagnino, the man behind

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.380
2017's Oscar-winning heartbreaker Call Me
By Your Name.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:25.720
The stylishly demented and utterly brilliant
remake of Suspiria wisely doesn't stray too

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.720
far from the original bewitching narrative.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.200
"Higher!

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:30.680
Higher!

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.180
Higher!

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:33.320
Higher!"

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.900
But Guadagnino manages to twist the formula
in shocking new directions, and he takes his

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.680
sweet time in letting this unholy tale unfold.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.310
The result is an epic 150 minutes of pure
horror hysterics, and according to Deadline,

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.760
it brought the Cannes crowd to their feet
for nearly eight full minutes.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:57.440
There was more than a little reason to worry
when Danny McBride and David Gordon Green

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.600
set out to "reboot" the Halloween franchise.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.990
As we learned more about their film, particularly
that it would ignore all of those ill-fated

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.780
sequels and remakes, our fears began to subside.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.880
"Wasn't it her brother who murdered all those
babysitters?"

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.599
"No, it was not her brother.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.720
That's something that people made up."

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:18.959
But the odds still seemed stacked against
the new Halloween living up to the original's

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:19.460
legacy.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.320
Not officially in competition at the 2018
Toronto International Film Festival, Halloween

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:29.739
instead had its premiere at a midnight screening,
playing to an eager crowd of film critics

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.060
and horror enthusiasts.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.840
According to the LA Times, that crowd met
the return of the Boogeyman, and a take-no-prisoners

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:40.569
turn from Jamie Lee Curtis, with the expected
amount of glee, giving the film a standing

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:44.080
ovation and helping launch it towards a record-breaking
box-office run.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:49.440
While Kristen Stewart became famous with starring
roles in big budget fluff like the Twilight

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:54.069
franchise, it's worth nothing that she built
her career by starring in indie flicks, and

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:55.760
continues to shine in the indie world.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:01.050
Personal Shopper, which made its premiere
at the Cannes Film Festival in early 2016,

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:05.560
is an eerily muted study of loss and grief
in the guise of a sexy paranormal thriller.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:10.080
Though the film's initial festival screening
met with a slightly divisive reaction from

00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:14.209
critics, the official premiere event was quite
a different scene, with Variety reporting

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:18.620
that the audience heaped some serious love
on Stewart, in the form of a lengthy standing

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:19.180
ovation.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.840
After scoring back-to-back hits with the formidable
one-two punch of Creed and Black Panther,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.400
Ryan Coogler has proven himself a rare director
capable of making big-budget films that all

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.500
but demand you stand up and cheer.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.670
Neither of those films, however, was his first
project to inspire a standing ovation alongside

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.640
his go-to actor, Michael B. Jordan.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.319
That honor belongs to 2013's "based-on-true-events"
drama Fruitvale Station.

00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:49.090
Part slice-of-life drama, and part damning
examination of police brutality, Coogler's

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:54.530
sobering film premiered at the 2013 Sundance
Film Festival, winning the hearts of festival

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.180
audiences, and a couple of the festival's
top prizes.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:02.019
Fruitvale Station's undeniable virtues were
further praised on the international stage

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:06.560
when it made its way to the Cannes and, according
to Color Lines, received a rousing standing

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.140
ovation.

